# CI policy: no push-triggered CI in this workspace

The owner of the AIC PIM workspace has explicitly removed every push-triggered
CI workflow (`cargo fmt --check`, `cargo build`, `cargo clippy`, `cargo test`,
JS test runners, etc.) from every repository under `c:\analog-pim\`. Push CI
is **not** part of the commit process and **must not** be re-introduced
without an explicit, in-conversation request from the user.

## What is forbidden

Do not, on your own initiative or as a side effect of "scaffolding" /
"cleanup" / "best practice":

1. Add or restore any GitHub Actions workflow file with a `push:` trigger
   that runs build / fmt / clippy / test / lint / typecheck / coverage on a
   project repo. (`name: ci`, `name: build`, `name: lint`, etc.)
2. Add or restore any workflow with a `pull_request:` trigger that runs the
   same gates — even if it doesn't trigger on push.
3. Add a `.git/hooks/pre-commit`, `.git/hooks/pre-push`, `.husky/`,
   `.cargo-husky` or `.pre-commit-config.yaml` that runs fmt / clippy /
   test / lint locally before commit or push. The whole purpose of the
   policy is that commits must be friction-free.
4. Re-add a `cargo fmt --check` / `prettier --check` / `eslint --max-warnings 0`
   gate anywhere that runs automatically on push or commit.

## What IS allowed

The policy is about *push-triggered gating*, not about the existence of
automation in general. The following continue to be fine:

1. **Release / publish workflows** triggered by tags or by
   `workflow_dispatch` only. Examples we keep:
   - `pim-orm/.github/workflows/publish.yml`
   - `pim-ui-kit/.github/workflows/publish.yml`
   - `pim-orm-offline/.github/workflows/publish.yml`
   - `pim-offline-server/.github/workflows/publish.yml`
   - `pim-offline-client/.github/workflows/release-windows-signed.yml`
   - `pim-offline-server/.github/workflows/release-windows-signed-binaries.yml`
   - `pim-offline-server/.github/workflows/ui-e2e.yml` (currently scheduled / manual; do not switch it back to `push`).
2. **Manual / `workflow_dispatch`-only** maintenance jobs (regenerate
   icons, sync core-assets, run a perf benchmark on demand). The
   triggering surface must be a button on the GitHub Actions tab — never
   `push` or `pull_request`.
3. **Local commands** the user runs explicitly (`cargo fmt`, `cargo
   clippy`, `cargo test`, `npm test`). Those are tools, not policy.

## When a CI gate would actually help

If during a session you notice a real reason a push-CI gate would be
useful — e.g. recurring regressions in one specific area — **stop and
ask the user**. Phrase it as a question, not a recommendation:

> "I notice we keep breaking X on commit. Would you like me to add a
>  `workflow_dispatch`-only check the team can run on demand, or are
>  you fine continuing without?"

Never volunteer "I added a CI check for this" after the fact.

## Default for new projects in the workspace

When the user (or you, on their behalf) creates a NEW Cargo / Tauri / Node
crate inside `c:\analog-pim\`, the default scaffold has:

- No `.github/workflows/ci.yml`.
- No pre-commit / pre-push hooks.
- A README that does NOT promise CI badges or "tests run on every push".

Release-only workflows (tag-triggered) may be added when the project
ships its first artefact, on the user's request.

## Rationale (so future agents understand the why)

The user wants a friction-free commit flow on a multi-repo workspace they
own end-to-end. CI failures on `cargo fmt --check`, transient flakes in
`cargo test`, and runner-image deprecations were creating a steady drip
of red badges that did not improve code quality but did interrupt
forward progress. They explicitly removed push CI to eliminate that
class of interruption. Re-adding it without permission would undo that
deliberate decision.
